"A heap object graph shows objects and references relevant to reachability or memory retention at a captured instant.\n\nUse when: Investigate why an object remains reachable. Explain a memory leak through a retaining path from a root.\n\nAvoid when: A domain example needs values but no memory-lifetime analysis.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote captured objects or intentionally aggregated object groups. Labeled arrows represent references through fields or collection entries. Root and weak-reference markers follow the selected runtime’s semantics.\n\nBuild: Record the runtime, snapshot source, and capture conditions. Choose the retained object and trace relevant paths toward roots. Annotate object identities and sizes only from the capture. Draw roots above retained objects, routing shared references around object labels.\n\nCheck: Reachability is not confused with exclusive ownership. Weak edges use the runtime’s actual retention meaning. Shallow and retained sizes are distinguished with units. Shared children and cycles stay readable without obscuring reference labels.\n\nPitfalls: Interpreting a large object as the leak without examining retention. Treating a synthetic object illustration as measured heap evidence.\n\nExample: A window listener retains a closure, which retains a large cache after its screen has been removed.\n\nAlternatives: Object diagram, Call graph."
